README-KERMIT -- five kermits, and which one to takeKermit moves files down a serial line to another machine.  There is noserial line under os9exec and no machine at the other end of it, so none ofthese can be demonstrated here -- what this file gives you is the choice,so that the one you copy onto real hardware is the right one.Short answer: take `ckermit' if you have the room.  It is C-Kermit5A(190), the real thing, with the whole protocol and a command language;it is 356K and that is the price.  Take `kermit' if you do not: 26K, needsno Microware module, and its full source is here.THE FIVE--------  file                bytes   cio   module     source   docs  ------------------------------------------------------------------  kermit              26828   no    kermit     yes      2 files  kermit2             21998   no    kermit2    --       1  kermit3             18148   no    Kermit     --       1  xkermit             14176   yes   xkermit    --       --  ckermit            356040   yes   wermit     --       1`ckermit' reports its own version when asked, which is how the line abovewas got rather than guessed:    C-Kermit 5A(190) BETA.14, 24 Jul 94    OS-9 Communications I/O, 5A(009) 9 Dec 93 for OS-9/68000    OS-9 file support, 5A(08) 01 Dec 92 for OS-9/68000    C-Kermit Protocol Module 5A(066), 24 Jul 94Measured 2026-09-01; `tools/datatests/net.cases' asserts it.TAKE THIS IF------------  ckermit    you want a kermit that a modern kermit will talk to without             argument, and a scripting language to drive it with.  Fourteen             times the size of the smallest one here.  kermit     you want one file that works and you have the source.  This is             the one to READ if you are porting.  kermit2    you want something between the two.  No source.  kermit3    smallest of the three plain ones.  Its module is called             `Kermit' with a capital K -- see the hazard below.  xkermit    only if the others do not suit: it needs `cio' and brings no             documentation.IF YOU CAN TAKE ONLY ONE FILE`kermit', 26,828 bytes, needs no Microware module.THE HAZARD IF YOU TAKE MORE THAN ONE------------------------------------OS-9 answers by MODULE NAME once a module is resident.  Two of these fivehave a module name that is NOT their filename:    kermit3    reports itself as `Kermit'    ckermit    reports itself as `wermit'`wermit' is C-Kermit's own historical name for its OS-9 build and is not amistake.  But it means that after `load ckermit' the module directory has a`wermit' in it, and `kermit3' and `kermit' can both be resident at oncewithout colliding -- while `Kermit' and `kermit' differ only in case, andOS-9's name comparison is case-insensitive.  DO NOT LOAD kermit3 AND kermitTOGETHER.  `tools/module_census.py' lists every such name on the disk.THE FLAGS THAT DECIDE WHETHER IT DOES WHAT YOU MEANTThese are not this collection's finding -- they are in the os9-dev notes andthey are worth repeating here, because neither errors:  l (line)   REQUIRED to aim at a device.  `kermit s /t1 file' sends over             the CONSOLE and silently ignores /t1; `kermit sl /t1 file'             uses the named device.  i (image)  for binary.  NOT `8', which is 8-bit quoting for a link that             is not 8-bit clean and corrupts data over one that is.  kermit sil /t1 mymodule      moves a module intactWHAT THEY WANT BESIDES THEMSELVESA serial device, which is the whole of it.  None of the five reads aconfiguration file on this disk.  `ckermit' will take a `.kermrc' if yougive it one; nothing here ships one.